additions.statement:
Fix any field K and let c_K(t) = 1 if c(t) = 1, else 0, in K. Problem 1 holds iff any of the following holds:
- the shifted sequences σⁿc_K = (c_K(n+t))_t, n ∈ ℕ, are linearly independent in K^ℕ;
- they span an infinite-dimensional subspace;
- the infinite Hankel matrix (c(i+j))_{i,j≥0} has infinite rank, i.e. the ranks of its n×n leading blocks are unbounded;
- c_K is not a torsion element of the K[X]-module K^ℕ with X acting as the shift (no nonzero Q ∈ K[X] has Q(σ)c_K = 0).

Problem 1 holds iff any of the following holds for x₂:
- the Engel expansion x₂ = 1/a_1 + 1/(a_1a_2) + … (a_k nondecreasing) is infinite;
- the Sylvester (greedy Egyptian-fraction) expansion is infinite;
- the factorial-base expansion x₂ = Σ_{k≥2} d_k/k! (0 ≤ d_k < k) does not terminate;
- the Lüroth expansion is not eventually periodic.

additions.proof_of_relation:
The subsets of ℕ definable in (ℕ, <) are exactly the finite and the cofinite sets, by quantifier elimination for (ℕ, <, 0, succ) or by an Ehrenfeucht–Fraïssé argument. So the statement is equivalent to 'c is not eventually constant', which is the proved Rule30Prize.center_not_eventually_constant.
This gives the bottom rung of a logic ladder:
- FO(<): proved;
- Presburger = MSO(ℕ,<) on unary sets: Problem 1 ([9], [36]);
- (ℕ,+,V_k): not k-automatic, stronger;
- Th(ℕ,+,S) undecidable: stronger still.

